window.docContents[156].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Building-Proof-Objects-Incrementally-_LPAR_Optional_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nsection IncrementalProofObjects\nset_option warn.sorry false\n\n\nAs you probably noticed while solving the exercises earlier in the\nchapter, constructing proof objects is more involved than\nconstructing the corresponding tactic proofs. Fortunately, there's\na simple trick to help in the construction: we can use the term\nsorry as placeholder of any type we like.As an example, let's walk through the process of constructing a\nproof object demonstrating the evenness of 8.theorem even_8_atmpt_1 : Even 8 := sorry\nMaybe we can use Even.succ_succ to construct a term of type Even 8?\nRecall that Even.succ_succ is of type∀ (n : Nat), Even n → Even (n + 2)\nIf we can demonstrate that 6 is even, we should be done.theorem even_8_atmpt_2 : Even 8 := Even.succ_succ 6 sorry\nIn the attempt above, we've omitted the proofs that 6 is even,\nbut it can easily be constructed following the same procedure:theorem even_8_atmpt_3 : Even 8 :=\n  Even.succ_succ 6 (Even.succ_succ 4 sorry)\n\ntheorem even_8_atmpt_4 : Even 8 :=\n  Even.succ_succ 6 (Even.succ_succ 4\n    (Even.succ_succ 2 sorry))\n\ntheorem even_8_atmpt_5 : Even 8 :=\n  Even.succ_succ 6 (Even.succ_succ 4\n    (Even.succ_succ 2 (Even.succ_succ 0 Even.zero)))\nTo recap, we've been guided by an informal proof that we have in\nour minds, and we check the high level details before completing\nthe intricacies of the proof. The sorry term allows us to do\nthis.\n\nend IncrementalProofObjects\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence","header":"10.4. Building Proof Objects Incrementally (Optional)","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Building-Proof-Objects-Incrementally-_LPAR_Optional_RPAR_"}});